<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	NO keychain-access-groups here, deliberately.

	`keychain-access-groups` is a restricted entitlement: on a Developer ID
	app the system only honours it when an embedded provisioning profile
	(Contents/embedded.provisionprofile) allowlists that exact group. This
	project has no such profile, and signing an app with the entitlement
	anyway does not degrade — AMFI refuses to spawn the process at all
	(`launchctl error 163`, "Security policy issue"). v3.3.0 shipped exactly
	that and could not be launched on any machine.

	Removing it is safe rather than merely expedient:
	ProfileKeychainDomainResolver decides the Keychain by *probing* at
	runtime, not by inspecting entitlements, so without the entitlement the
	probe returns errSecMissingEntitlement and profile credentials use the
	login Keychain — the behaviour every shipped release before v3.3.0 had.

	To restore data-protection Keychain storage, all three of these are
	required together, and none of them alone is sufficient:
	  1. A Developer ID provisioning profile carrying the Keychain Sharing
	     capability for `<team>.com.claudeusagetracker.credentials`.
	  2. That profile embedded at Contents/embedded.provisionprofile by the
	     release workflow before signing.
	  3. This entitlement restored, matching the profile's group exactly.
	scripts/validate_distribution.sh enforces (1)+(2) whenever (3) is
	present, so the combination that broke v3.3.0 cannot be committed again.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
